  "final_answer": "ndidate later.\n2. **`agent/b`:** merge first. It is one commit, touches one file, and has a clean worktree.\n3. **`agent/a`:** merge second. It is two commits, also disjoint and clean.\n4. **`main`:** merge last, or first if you want its `m.txt` commit as the base. Once `main` is in, `agent/release` is the superset.\n\nIf you converge with `/GitConverge agent/release`, it merges `main` first, then `agent/a`, then `agent/b`. Because the changed files are disjoint, the result should be the same either way.\n\n## Why\n\n- Merge order barely matters here, because there is no file overlap and no ancestry dependency between a, b and main.\n- I put the smaller, single-purpose lane first to keep each step easy to review and revert.\n- Use merge commits (`--no-ff`), not rebase or squash. The lanes are only local, but a later ancestry-based cleanup needs real merge history.\n\n## Blocked or unknown\n\n- **Integration target:** please confirm it is `agent/release` and not `main`. If it is `main`, `agent/release`, `agent/a` and `agent/b` are the lanes to merge in, and `main` has no unpushed-commit problem to resolve first.\n- **Review and validation:** none exist for any lane. Tests and checks are not identified.\n- **Remote freshness:** unknown. Run `/GitRecommend ... --remote` to refresh refs.\n- **`wt-b` ownership:** not recorded. Don't delete `agent/b` or its worktree until it is merged.\n- **Next step:** to apply this, run `/GitConverge agent/release` for a preview, then `--apply`. I changed nothing.",
